<.part title="Boards, lists and cards" id="boards-basics">
  <p>
    A board is a set of lists (columns), and each list holds cards that you drag
    between and within lists. Every board also has a short <strong>code</strong>, such
    as <code>qvm-v1-rem</code>, that names it in links, the API and the CLI.
  </p>
  <p>A card can carry:</p>
  <ul class="list-disc space-y-1 pl-5">
    <li>a Markdown description, a checklist and comments</li>
    <li>a priority from low to critical, a start date and a due date</li>
    <li>flags: flagged, blocked, needs review, waiting, starred, stuck</li>
    <li>tags, one or more assignees, a cover colour, web links and attachments</li>
    <li>a timer, with time spent against an estimate</li>
  </ul>
  <p id="help-flag-attention">
    A flag says who clears it. <strong>Filled</strong>, with a solid icon (and a
    raised hand on the open card), means nothing on the board will clear it: a
    person has to act. <strong>Outline only</strong>, and <em>clears itself</em>
    on the open card, means something already in motion will: a blocked card
    whose blocker is still open is unblocked when that card is done, and a card
    awaiting verification on a board with a verify pool is decided by a verifier
    agent. Waiting, flagged and stuck always need a person; a starred card asks
    nothing of anyone. Hover a flag to see who clears it.
  </p>
  <p>
    <code>@name</code> in a description or comment mentions someone who can see the
    board, and they get an email (and a desktop notification, if they have those on). Lists can have a colour, a WIP limit, and their own
    sort order or grouping.
  </p>
  <p id="help-card-authors">
    Under a card's properties, <strong>Created by</strong>
    says who made it and <strong>Last changed by</strong>
    who touched it last (an edit, a move, a comment, a
    checklist item, an attachment or a flag) and when. That can be a person, a runner
    loop by its runner's name ("Dev server loop"), an agent by its token's label, or
    an automation rule by its name. When nobody was recorded, as on cards older than
    this, only the time is shown.
  </p>
</.part>

<.part title="Subcards and epics" id="boards-subcards">
  <p>
    Any card can have subcards. It becomes a board of its own, with lists from a
    template, and those subcards can have subcards too, to any depth. A common pattern
    is for top-level cards to be <strong>epics</strong> and their subcards to be the actual tasks.
  </p>
  <p>
    Each card summarises everything beneath it: how many tasks are done, the earliest
    start and latest due date, whether anything below is blocked or overdue, and a
    health state. <.app_link current_user={@current_user} to={~p"/work"}>My work</.app_link>
    lists everything assigned to you across every board and level, after what is waiting
    on you: the overseer's alerts first, when a board has it on (Automations, Overseer).
    Open cards with no due date aren't listed there; its header says how many there are,
    and completed ones are under Show completed. The API, the CLI, the MCP tools and the
    AI chat still see every assigned card.
  </p>
  <p>
    Cards can also <strong>depend</strong>
    on each other ("blocked by" and "blocks"). A card waiting on unfinished work shows a
    blocked badge.
  </p>
</.part>

<.part title="Waiting on you" id="boards-waiting">
  <p>
    <.app_link current_user={@current_user} to={~p"/work"}>My work</.app_link>
    opens with everything that needs you, oldest first: questions waiting on you, cards
    flagged for your review, failed runner jobs, cards in progress with nobody on them and
    Scheduled cards not started by the end of their day, on the boards you own. The header counts them on every page.
  </p>
  <p>
    The boards show them too. An epic's card carries <strong>⏸ 2 waiting on you</strong>
    for what in its subcards is on you, and a line naming each with its reason, such as <em>Waiting on you: #2063 (a question), #2075 (3 rejections)</em>. Questions waiting on
    other people show in the line only (<em>and 1 on someone else</em>), never in the count. Click the
    chip to open the sub-board showing only those cards. Open the epic and its subcard list
    marks the same rows: each one waiting on you carries a badge with its reason (<em>to verify</em>, <em>a question</em>),
    one waiting on someone else a quieter mark naming who it waits on, and the line's #ids above the list jump to their rows.
    A card further down marks the subcard it sits under, with its own #id. The board's header says
    <strong>Waiting on you: N</strong>
    for the whole board and opens My work kept to it. <code>slipdock card</code>
    and <code>slipdock board</code>
    print the same line.
  </p>
  <p>
    A question addressed to you also raises an alert in the header that only you see, and
    an email, and so does a card of yours past its board's rejection limit. Turn either off in <.app_link
      current_user={@current_user}
      to={~p"/account/settings"}
    >account settings</.app_link>, under <strong>When something waits on you</strong>.
  </p>
  <p>
    Some of them you won't act on. The <strong>×</strong>
    on a row dismisses it from your own list. It stays off for that reason only: a second
    failed job, a new question or the flag put on again brings the card back. To keep a
    whole board quiet, choose <strong>Mute on my Waiting on you</strong>
    in the board's <strong>…</strong>
    menu. Nothing from that board or any board under it shows on your list, and a strip on
    the board says it is muted. Muting is not archiving: the board works as before, for
    you and everyone else.
  </p>
  <p>
    Both are yours alone. Nobody else's list changes. The list always says what it is
    hiding (<em>3 waiting · 2 dismissed · 1 board muted</em>), and <strong>Show</strong>
    lists them with <strong>Undo</strong>
    and <strong>Unmute</strong>. From the CLI it is <code>slipdock work --dismiss</code>, <code>--dismissed</code>, <code>--undismiss</code>,
    <code>--mute</code>
    and <code>--unmute</code>.
  </p>
</.part>

<.part title="Desktop notifications" id="boards-desktop-notifications">
  <p>
    Slipdock can show a notification on your computer when a card starts waiting on you,
    a question is routed to you, or somebody <code>@</code>mentions you: the same things
    that email you, which they still do. Turn on
    <strong>Desktop notifications on this browser</strong>
    under <.app_link current_user={@current_user} to={~p"/account/settings"}>Account → Preferences</.app_link>;
    the browser asks whether to allow them. It is off until you do.
  </p>
  <p>
    They come through a Slipdock tab you have open, in the background too, and with
    several tabs open you get one, not one each. With no tab open nothing shows, on
    purpose: Slipdock doesn't reach you when you have closed it. Clicking one brings the
    tab forward and opens the card. You get none for what you did yourself, nor while
    you are looking at that card.
  </p>
  <p>
    The setting is kept in this browser, because the browser's permission is too: turn it
    on in each one you use. If notifications are blocked for the site, or the browser
    can't show them, the toggle says so; unblock them in the browser's site settings.
    Only the web app shows them; the API, the CLI and agents have nothing to set.
  </p>
</.part>

<.part title="Views" id="boards-views">
  <p>
    The same cards, shown different ways. Switch between them from the view menu, or press <kbd class="kbd kbd-xs">v</kbd>:
  </p>
  <ul class="list-disc space-y-1 pl-5">
    <li><strong>Board</strong>: lists side by side.</li>
    <li>
      <strong>Swimlanes</strong>: a grid with any attribute on each axis, such as assignee by priority.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Table</strong>: a sortable row per card, with editing in place and CSV export.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Timeline</strong>: a Gantt chart by start and due date, with dependency lines.
    </li>
    <li><strong>Calendar</strong>: a month or week, by due date.</li>
    <li><strong>Outline</strong>: the board as a tree of cards and subcards.</li>
    <li>
      <strong>Narrative</strong>: what happened to the cards over a date range, told as prose.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Prioritise</strong>: a ranked table where priority, votes and scores are edited in place.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    The filter bar narrows any view by text, tag, priority, flag, due date and origin (who
    or what made the card: a person, the sweep, an agent's job…). A view
    with its filters can be saved, shared, or published as a public link.
  </p>
  <p id="boards-search-results">
    On the board, a search or filter shows its matches as one list down the page, grouped by
    the list each card is in, in board order, so none sit off the side of the screen. Each
    heading gives the list's name and how many match; click it to fold or unfold the group.
    Done, and any list with more than 10 matches, starts folded, and the lists with no
    matches are named on one line at the end. Click a row to open the card, or use its
    arrow to move it to another list. <strong>Board</strong>
    in the banner shows the matches in their columns instead, for that search;
    <strong>Clear</strong>
    brings the whole board back.
  </p>
</.part>

<.part title="Card views" id="boards-card-views">
  <p>
    Choose what you see of a card: each part of its front, each section of the opened card
    and each property and group of its sidebar, assignees and dates included. Card views are yours alone and kept on the server, so
    they follow you to every browser. Nobody else's cards change.
  </p>
  <ul class="list-disc space-y-1 pl-5">
    <li>
      <strong>Levels</strong>: global, then a board, then one list on it. Each states only
      what it changes, <em>Show</em> or <em>Hide</em>, and inherits the rest from the level below.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Named card views</strong>: save a set under a name and apply it at any level,
      with that level's own choices on top. A saved view can name one too, under
      Display → <em>Card view for this view</em>.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Show all</strong>: a switch in the Display menu that shows every part of the
      card front on this board while it is on.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>The handoff note</strong>: show <em>Handoff note (2 lines)</em>
      or <em>Handoff note in full</em>
      to read where a card was left without opening it.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Open them from the Display menu's <strong>Card views…</strong>, from
    <strong>Card view…</strong>
    in a list's ⋯ menu, or at <.app_link
      current_user={@current_user}
      to={~p"/card-views"}
    >Card views</.app_link>. The board's own settings always win: a card view never brings back
    what a simple board or a list hides. The list level applies on the board and swimlanes;
    the timeline, calendar and outline use the board's.
  </p>
</.part>

<.part title="Planning" id="boards-planning">
  <ul class="list-disc space-y-1 pl-5">
    <li>
      <strong>Sprints</strong>: a sprint board where every card is a sprint, with burndown
      and velocity charts.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Roadmaps</strong>: give lists a category (to do, in progress, done, dropped,
      triage, scheduled, housekeeping)
      and a horizon such as Q1 2027; add milestones; colour any view by list, priority,
      health, assignee or tag.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Recurring cards</strong>: <em>Repeat…</em>
      on a card (or <code>every: mon</code>
      in quick add) makes it repeat: daily, weekdays, weekly, monthly on a date or the
      last Friday, yearly, or some days after the last one is done. The next one is a copy
      of the current one, made in your timezone, and one still open has its due date moved
      on rather than another piling up. On a board with a <strong>Scheduled</strong>
      list (a list in the <em>Scheduled</em>
      category; the Slipdock and Simple templates have one, after To Do) the next one is
      made there unless you chose a list: recurring agent work waits there for the runners'
      rules to send it on its day, out of your To Do, and nobody picks from it. One still
      there after its day is on your <em>Waiting on you</em>. <em>Duplicate</em>
      makes the same copy by hand.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Snooze</strong>: <em>Snooze…</em>
      on a card, or <kbd>z</kbd>
      over it on the board (<kbd>z</kbd> <kbd>z</kbd>
      is tomorrow), hides a card that isn't actionable yet until a day, then brings it back
      at the top of its list. Each list's foot counts what is snoozed in it and shows them,
      faded, on a click. <code>snooze: mon</code>
      in quick add does it as you add the card.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Tidy up…</strong>
      in the board's menu looks the board, and every board beneath it, over for rot: epics
      whose work is all done but still open, cards in Done never ticked off, cards stuck in
      progress, epics with no next step, long-overdue cards, likely duplicates, wiki pages
      nothing links to and links with a typo. Each comes with the evidence and a proposed fix
      to apply or dismiss; a dismissal is remembered, and an applied fix can be undone.
      Proposed handbook edits on one section say which others share it, and apply one after
      the other, each as its own lines. Board
      settings → <em>Gardening</em>
      picks the checks and how long before something counts.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Findings</strong>: what agents' passes learned on a board, and what went wrong
      (a rejection, a job that timed out). Triage and the server handle them; what waits on
      you is a rule drafted from one that recurs. Its tile on the board's
      <em>Agentic Building</em>
      page counts those proposals and opens the board's Findings page (a card's Findings link opens it too),
      which leads with them, each linking the Tidy-up page to apply or dismiss. Below, for
      reference, the findings: filter by status, kind, source, card or date, and promote a
      finding into the handbook, merge repeats or dismiss it. A finding a waiting proposal
      names is <em>waiting on a person</em>, out of the open list (its own filter), since
      deciding it would close that proposal. A card with open findings
      says how many, and links there.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Ledger</strong>: what shipped on a board since 07:00 yesterday, a line per card
      completed, accepted, merged or deployed, with who accepted it, its evidence, the
      <em>should</em>
      rules it missed, where its time went and what its jobs spent, grouped by day. Its
      tile on the board's <em>Agentic Building</em>
      page opens it; <em>Look closer</em>
      opens a card with its chat. Ten minutes' reading instead of approving each card.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Batched releases</strong>: Agentic Building → <em>Releases</em>
      on the root board chooses how merged work reaches production: with each merge, or in
      batches, with an age cap in hours for how long the oldest merge may wait. A merged
      card is still done, and <em>Release now</em>
      is how a person ships it; on a batched board it shows <em>merged, awaiting release</em>
      until a deploy ships it, then <em>released</em>
      with the time. A release runs when a card of priority <em>critical</em>
      merges, when the oldest merge reaches the age cap, or when the board's owner presses <em>Release now</em>, on the board's release banner or on that page. The banner's
      <em>merged, awaiting release</em>
      count opens the list of the cards the next release takes. Releases run one at a
      time: a runner runs the board's release command, and anything asked for meanwhile
      becomes one follow-up. While one runs, the banner's <em>follow the build</em>
      opens the run it started. A runner runs the default <code>gh workflow run</code>
      command itself; any other runs only if that machine's <code>local.json</code>
      lists it, word for word, under <code>release_commands</code>. A red release (its suite,
      build or deploy failing) holds releases: the banner says why, with the failing run,
      and a critical fix card goes on top of the ready list. Its merge lifts the hold and
      releases; the owner can also press <em>Lift hold</em>, which releases nothing.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Who gets asked</strong>: Agentic Building → <em>Who gets asked</em>
      names a person for each kind of question an agent asks (product, UX, security,
      release, anything else). Their waits go to that person, and to the board's owner
      when nobody is named. Beside it, <em>An approval's proposal stands after</em>
      (off until set; 4h, say) lets an unanswered wait of kind other that offers
      choices and proposes one go ahead with its proposal: the card says so and the
      person asked is told. Product, UX, security and release questions, and a wait
      that proposes nothing, always wait for a person.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Profiles</strong>: one word for what kind of change a card is (security,
      schema, feature, fix, docs or spike), off until Agentic Building → <em>Profiles</em>
      turns them on. An agent starting a card with none gets one from its paths and tags,
      strictest first; the card's front shows it as a chip, and the card panel's <em>Profile</em>
      select sets or clears it. When an agent completes the card, the profile is checked
      again against what the change touched, and raised, never lowered: a migration or a
      security path raises even one you set, and more than 400 changed lines makes an
      agent's or an inferred fix or docs a feature (a fix you set keeps it). <em>Human tier, by profile</em>, on the same page, says
      whether an agent's acceptance merges the card, merges it onto the ledger or holds it
      for a person: by default schema and security changes wait for you. A spike merges nothing:
      its prototype stays on its branch, and it holds no lane.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Incidents</strong>: when a card broke something that shipped, its <em>Incident</em>
      line, under Dependencies, names the card that shipped it and the check that should
      have caught it (bar, rules, CI, verify, audit or none). The causing card then shows
      the incident it caused, and carries a finding naming that check.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Status updates</strong>: on track, at risk or off track, with a note, shown
      beside the health the board works out for itself. On a card agents work, the board
      posts each step itself (Writing code, Verifying, Merged, Shipped to production…),
      quietly, at the top of the card; a finished card shows there how it ended (done when,
      accepted by whom, what merged and shipped) instead of a next step. With delivery
      metrics on, a merged card that no succeeded deploy names after 20 minutes (the
      board's <em>Not shipped after</em>
      setting) shows an amber <em>Merged 1a634c6 · not shipped after 45m</em>
      and raises one warning alert on the board, whether or not the overseer is on; the
      deploy that names it clears both. On a board with batched releases the minutes count
      from the card's release, not its merge, so a card waiting for its batch raises
      nothing. How long ago each
      one was ("18 min ago") keeps up with the clock while the card is open.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Custom fields</strong>
      (numbers, ratings, choices, dates, text) and formula fields with RICE and ICE presets.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Budget voting</strong>: everyone spends a set number of votes across the board.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Typed links</strong>
      between cards on any board, so a goal card can collect the work contributing to it.
    </li>
  </ul>
</.part>

<.part title="Getting around fast" id="boards-fast">
  <ul class="list-disc space-y-1 pl-5">
    <li>
      <strong>Quick add</strong>: press <kbd class="kbd kbd-xs">c</kbd>
      or use the header box and type a line such as "call the printers about the banners
      friday, urgent". It becomes a card on your default board. After a semicolon comes the
      card's description, after two a comment: "Fix the login; seen on Safari only;; ask Sam first".
    </li>
    <li>
      <kbd class="kbd kbd-xs">Ctrl</kbd>-<kbd class="kbd kbd-xs">P</kbd>
      opens the command palette, and <kbd class="kbd kbd-xs">Ctrl</kbd>-<kbd class="kbd kbd-xs">O</kbd>
      opens any card by its title or number. Press <kbd class="kbd kbd-xs">?</kbd>
      for every keyboard shortcut.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Favourites</strong>: heart a board, list, card or saved view to keep it two
      taps away.
    </li>
    <li id="help-select-mode">
      <strong>Select</strong>
      in the board's toolbar picks out cards to change all at once:
      click a card to pick it, <kbd class="kbd kbd-xs">Shift</kbd>-click another to pick
      every card between the two (down a list, then across), or press the tick in a list's
      header to pick every card it shows (again to let them go). <strong>Select all</strong>
      in a list's ⋯ menu turns select mode on with every card the list shows picked; in
      select mode it adds them to what is already picked, and never lets any go.
    </li>
    <li>
      On a phone, the board becomes one list at a time, with navigation and quick add in thumb reach.
    </li>
  </ul>
</.part>

<.part title="Templates, moving and archiving" id="boards-moving">
  <ul class="list-disc space-y-1 pl-5">
    <li>
      <.app_link current_user={@current_user} to={~p"/templates"}>Templates</.app_link>
      are named sets of lists for new boards and sub-boards.
    </li>
    <li>
      A card moves to another board with its subcards, from the same menu that moves it between lists.
    </li>
    <li>
      Archiving a card or a whole board puts it away without deleting anything, and it can be restored.
    </li>
    <li>
      Boards can be imported from Trello or another Slipdock server, and everything you
      have can be exported, under <.app_link current_user={@current_user} to={~p"/account/data"}>Account → Import & export</.app_link>.
    </li>
  </ul>
</.part>
